<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frans Post</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1612">1612</a>–<a href="/years/1680">1680</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frans Post was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> painter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a>, the first European artist to paint landscapes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. He travelled to Dutch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-maurits-of-nassau-siegen">Johan Maurits of Nassau-Siegen</a> and worked there from 1637 to 1644. After returning to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> he continued for decades to paint Brazilian scenes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> mills, plantations and river valleys from his sketches.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frans-post.jpg" alt="Frans Post" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frans Hals · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frans_Hals_-_Frans_Post_(Worcester_Art_Museum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frans Janszoon Post was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a>, the son of Jan Janszoon Post, a glass painter trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a>, and Francijntje Verbraken. His elder brother was Pieter Post, one of the leading architects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> classicism. Little is known of his life before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. He most likely trained first with his father and brother, and probably also with a Dutch master; the Post scholar Erik Larsen believes this was the landscape painter Pieter de Molijn, though not everyone accepts it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frans-hals">Frans Hals</a> painted his portrait.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Post probably won a place at court through his brother's connections, and was encouraged to travel by Johan Maurits van Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>, the governor of Dutch Brazil, at a time when plague had broken out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a>. In Brazil he made many sketches and etchings but finished only six paintings, dated 1637 to 1640, which Maurits later presented to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> in 1679. They show recognisable places, most with water, Brazilian plants and sometimes animals in the foreground, perhaps inspired by his travelling companion, the naturalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-marcgraf">Georg Marcgraf</a>, under grey skies that fill half the canvas. Another of Maurits's artists, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-eckhout">Albert Eckhout</a>, recorded the native people.</p><p>Back in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, Post joined the Haarlem Guild of St. Luke in 1646 and served as an officer in 1656–7 and 1658. In 1650 he married Jannetje Bogaert, daughter of Professor Salomon Bogaert. His Brazilian scenes painted in Haarlem grew more imaginary the further he was from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, with brighter colours, dark framing foliage and idealised baroque compositions. Nearly all include groups of people dancing or working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> mills, typically enslaved, who become part of the landscape rather than its subject.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Post produced about 140 paintings, nearly half of them dated, and the last dated work is from 1669. Since no dated paintings survive from the 1670s, he seems to have stopped about ten years before his death; Whitehead and Boeseman claim he developed an alcohol problem. His two sons died before him and his daughter shortly after. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a> and was buried in the St. Bavochurch on 17 February 1680. His works were widely collected in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and present an idealised vision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonial rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frans Post” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He brought the painters <b>Frans Post</b> and Albert Eckhout and the naturalists Georg Marcgraf and Willem Piso, whose work was the first scientific study of Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-brazil">Dutch Brazil</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.38,4.64&amp;year=1656" aria-label="Haarlem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Frans Post"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5242 5641h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5242 5641h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.38,4.64&amp;year=1656">What was here in 1656? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
